{"records":[{"id":"z4m2d88hgz","title":"Part2_Petromurin C induces protective autophagy and apoptosis in FLT3-ITD-positive AML: synergy with gilteritinib","description":"Treatment of acute myeloid leukemia (AML) remains inefficient due to drug resistance and relapse, particularly in patients with FMS-like tyrosine kinase 3 (FLT3)-internal tandem duplication (ITD). Marine-derived natural products have recently been used for drug development against AML. We show here that petromurin C, isolated from the culture extract of the marine-derived fungus Aspergillus candidus KUFA0062, which was isolated from the marine sponge Epipolasis sp., induces early autophagy followed by apoptotic cell death via activation of the intrinsic cell death pathway concomitant with mitochondrial stress and downregulation of Mcl-1 in FLT3-ITD mutated MV4-11 cells. Moreover, petromurin C synergized with the clinically-used FLT3 inhibitor gilteritinib at sub-toxic concentrations. Altogether our results suggest that petromurin C could act as an anti-leukemic agent alone or in combination with gilteritinib.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-12-13T11:12:18.026Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/z4m2d88hgz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/z4m2d88hgz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"You Na ","last_name":"Ha","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Health Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xzjmk67vnc","title":"Part3_Petromurin C induces protective autophagy and apoptosis in FLT3-ITD-positive AML: synergy with gilteritinib","description":"Treatment of acute myeloid leukemia (AML) remains inefficient due to drug resistance and relapse, particularly in patients with FMS-like tyrosine kinase 3 (FLT3)-internal tandem duplication (ITD). Marine-derived natural products have recently been used for drug development against AML. We show here that petromurin C, isolated from the culture extract of the marine-derived fungus Aspergillus candidus KUFA0062, which was isolated from the marine sponge Epipolasis sp., induces early autophagy followed by apoptotic cell death via activation of the intrinsic cell death pathway concomitant with mitochondrial stress and downregulation of Mcl-1 in FLT3-ITD mutated MV4-11 cells. Moreover, petromurin C synergized with the clinically-used FLT3 inhibitor gilteritinib at sub-toxic concentrations. Altogether our results suggest that petromurin C could act as an anti-leukemic agent alone or in combination with gilteritinib.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-12-13T11:12:45.533Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xzjmk67vnc","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xzjmk67vnc"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"You Na ","last_name":"Ha","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Health Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9dhbprzxh2","title":"Part1_Petromurin C induces protective autophagy and apoptosis in FLT3-ITD-positive AML: synergy with gilteritinib","description":"Treatment of acute myeloid leukemia (AML) remains inefficient due to drug resistance and relapse, particularly in patients with FMS-like tyrosine kinase (FLT)3-internal tandem duplication (ITD). Marine-derived natural products have recently been used for drug development against AML. We show here that petromurin C, isolated from the culture extract of the marine-derived fungus Aspergillus candidus KUFA0062, which was isolated from the marine sponge Epipolasis sp., induces early autophagy followed by apoptotic cell death via activation of the intrinsic cell death pathway concomitant with mitochondrial stress and downregulation of Mcl-1 in FLT3-ITD mutated MV4-11 cells. Moreover, petromurin C synergized with the clinically-used FLT3 inhibitor gilteritinib at sub-toxic concentrations. Altogether our results suggest that petromurin C could act as an anti-leukemic agent alone or in combination with gilteritinib.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-12-13T11:09:36.344Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9dhbprzxh2","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9dhbprzxh2"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"You Na ","last_name":"Ha","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Health Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"mg36rwrkb7","title":"Acute Undifferentiated Leukemia ","description":"To explore the diagnostic criteria of acute undifferentiated leukemia (AUL) with FLT3 missense mutations,","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-08-03T14:13:58.374Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/mg36rwrkb7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/mg36rwrkb7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"huiyang","last_name":"deng","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Case Report"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"3df2cj3v9z","title":"AMI-M1","description":"AML-M1 (Acute Myeloid Leukemia, French-American-British Classification M1)\nDefinition:\nAML-M1 is a subtype of acute myeloid leukemia characterized by a proliferation of myeloblasts (immature white blood cells) with minimal maturation. It is classified under the FAB (French-American-British) system as M1, which means “without maturation.”\n\nKey Features:\n\nBone marrow and blood contain ≥ 20% myeloblasts with minimal differentiation into mature cells.\n\nMyeloblasts typically lack significant granulocytic maturation beyond the blast stage.\n\nCommon markers: CD13, CD33 are usually positive, HLA-DR often positive.\n\nClinical symptoms: fatigue, anemia, infections, bleeding tendencies due to bone marrow failure.\n\nDiagnosis:\n\nBone marrow biopsy showing ≥ 20% myeloblasts.\n\nImmunophenotyping showing expression of myeloid markers such as CD13, CD33.\n\nCytogenetic and molecular testing to identify mutations (e.g., FLT3, NPM1) that guide prognosis and treatment.\n\nTreatment:\n\nTypically involves intensive chemotherapy aimed at inducing remission.\n\nMay include consolidation chemotherapy and hematopoietic stem cell transplantation in eligible patients.\n\nPrognosis:\n\nPrognosis depends on various factors including genetic mutations, patient age, and response to treatment.\n\nAML-M1 has a variable prognosis; patients with favorable mutations have better outcomes.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-07-07T17:10:45.234Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/3df2cj3v9z","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/3df2cj3v9z"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Eldirdiri Fadol Ibrahim","last_name":"Ibrahim","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Hematological Cancer","Chemical Treatment","Science, Technology, Engineering and Mathematics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"5pkdjgmwsc","title":"SWATH-MS analysis raw datasets: protein expression in OCI-AML3 and MV4-11 cells","description":"These raw datasets illustrate protein expression in both OCI-AML3 and MV4-11 cells in baseline, FLT3-stimulated, or FLT3-inhibited conditions. ","source":{"id":"c41270fd-87bf-4be5-8668-b8aa8f867463","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-08-17T12:46:58.317Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/5pkdjgmwsc","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/5pkdjgmwsc"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Livia","last_name":"Gozzellino","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Mass Spectrometry","Quizartinib"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"26956f94vz","title":"RT-PCR (Gene Expression Profiling of Key Oncogenes and Tumor Suppressor Genes in Leukemia)","description":"This study investigates the expression profiles of key oncogenes (FLT3, MYC, CXCR4, KIT) and tumor suppressor genes (TP53, RUNX1, BCR-ABL1) in leukemia patients compared to healthy controls. Using RT-qPCR, significant dysregulation of these genes was observed, highlighting their potential roles in leukemia progression. The findings suggest these genes as promising biomarkers for early diagnosis and therapeutic targets. Statistical analysis revealed significant correlations between specific genes, providing insights into their interactions in leukemia. This research contributes to understanding the molecular mechanisms of leukemia and supports the development of targeted therapies for improved patient outcomes.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-03-03T01:05:26.355Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/26956f94vz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/26956f94vz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Amir","last_name":"Jalali","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Saja Jawad Obaid ","last_name":"Al-Sailawi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Basma ","last_name":"Maytham Oleiwi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sura ","last_name":"Hasan Hadi","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Targeted Therapy","Early Diagnosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"56r624b4v2","title":"Defining the role of HIF-2alpha in the pathogenesis of acute myeloid leukemia and exploiting its inhibition as a new therapeutic approach to promote leukemia differentiation and exhaustion","description":"This dataset represents findings obtained within a project supported by the Italian Ministry of Health (project code: RF-2019-12369841) and focused on investigating the mechanism of myeloid differentiation blockade by the transcription factor HIF-2alpha in acute myeloid leukemia (AML) and the consequences of its inhibition with novel targeted therapies.\nWe found that in AML HIF-2alpha regulates a specific set of target genes involved in transcriptional repression, leading to inhibition of entire gene clusters linked to myeloid difefrentiation. As a consequence, HIF-2alpha blockade via genetic or pharmacologic approaches leads to myeloid differentiation and leukemia debulking. Also, we positioned HIF-2alpha under direct transcriptional control by the prodifferentiation agent all-trans retinoic acid (ATRA) and demonstrated that HIF-2alpha blockade cooperates with ATRA to trigger AML cell differentiation.\nLinks related to this dataset contain: 1. A publication describing part of the results funded by this grant; 2. Source data linked to this publication and deposited in the BioStudies repository (accession number: S-SCDT-10_15252-EMMM_202317810); 3. Sequencing data deposited in the GEO repository.","source":{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-06-11T06:49:50.887Z","url":"https://ordr.hsr.it/datasets/56r624b4v2","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/56r624b4v2"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Rosa","last_name":"Bernardi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Pier Paolo","last_name":"Pandolfi","orcid":"0000-0002-5352-5295"},{"first_name":"Massimo","last_name":"Bernardi","orcid":"0000-0002-1334-0851"},{"first_name":"Daniela","last_name":"Magliulo","orcid":"0000-0003-3363-2736"},{"first_name":"Matilde","last_name":"Simoni","orcid":"0000-0002-5631-6763"},{"first_name":"Marco","last_name":"Morelli","orcid":"0000-0003-1862-667X"}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/00789fa95","name":"Ministero della Salute","grant_id":"RF-2019-12369841"}],"categories":["Adult Myeloid Leukemia","Targeted Therapy","Acute Myeloid Leukemia Differentiation","Myeloid Differentiation","Hypoxia Signalling"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"55v76yw529","title":"Deubiquitinases: Pro-oncogenic Activity and Therapeutic Targeting in Acute Leukemia","description":"Dysregulation of kinase signaling pathways via mutations favors tumor cell survival and resistance to therapy and it is common in cancer. In our work we reveal a novel mechanism of posttranslational regulation of kinase signaling and nuclear receptor activity via deubiquitination in acute leukemia. We observed that the ubiquitin specific protease 7 (USP7) forms a complex with USP11 to deubiquitinate oncogenic lymphocyte cell-specific protein tyrosine kinase (LCK). Deubiquitination of LCK controls its activity, thereby altering T cell receptor signaling. Impairment of LCK activity leads to increased expression of the glucocorticoid receptor (GR) transcript, culminating into transcriptional activation of pro-apoptotic target genes and sensitizes cells to glucocorticoids in primary T cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (T-ALL) patient samples. The transcriptional activation of pro-apoptotic target genes, such as BCL2L11, is orchestrated by the deubiquitinase activity and mediated via an increase in enhancer-promoter interaction intensity. Our data unveil how dysregulated deubiquitination controls signaling pathways, leading to cancer cell survival and drug non-response, and suggest novel therapeutic combinations towards targeting leukemia.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-11-16T20:51:22.317Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/55v76yw529","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/55v76yw529"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Blanca","last_name":"Gutierrez","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry","Protein-Tyrosine Kinase","Ubiquitylation","Acute Leukemia"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hx4nwr8yyd","title":"Data for: Therapy-related acute myeloid leukemia developing 14 years after allogeneic stem cell transplantation, from a persistent R882H-DNMT3A mutated clone of patient origin.","description":"Supplementary (DOCX  2.34MB): \nMaterials and methods. \nFig. S1. TP53 mutation found in the lung carcinoma cells taken from the patient in 2012. \nFig. S2. Karyotype illustrating the metaphases of the 2015 therapy-related myelodysplastic syndrome cells. \nFig. S3. Paint of chromosome 15 (green) by fluorescent in situ hybridization. \nFig. S4. Analysis of the chimerism (Mentype® Chimera® software, Biotype) at 2015 therapy-related acute myeloid leukemia diagnosis. \nFig. S5. IDH1, DNMT3A and NPM1 mutations found at the time of the acute myeloid leukemia diagnosis in 2001. \nFig. S6. DNMT3A and TP53 mutations found at the time of the therapy-related acute myeloid leukemia diagnosis in 2015. \nFig. S7. Standard curve for the ASO-qPCR. \nFig. S8. Sensitivity assay for quantitative ASO-qPCR. \n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-07-18T12:28:12.179Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hx4nwr8yyd","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hx4nwr8yyd"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Iván","last_name":"Martín","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Felipe J","last_name":"Chaves","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rosario","last_name":"Abellán","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Francisca","last_name":"García","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mar","last_name":"Tormo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Carlos","last_name":"Solano","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Juan-Carlos","last_name":"Hernandez-Boluda","orcid":null},{"first_name":"María Dolores","last_name":"Olivares","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Eva","last_name":"Villamón","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Fernando","last_name":"Domingo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Blanca","last_name":"Navarro","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Marisa","last_name":"Calabuig","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Paula","last_name":"Amat","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alicia","last_name":"Serrano","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Molecular Biology","Cytogenetics"],"journal_issn":["00144800"]}],"total_records":125349,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":164,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":125349,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":124787,"restricted":61,"embargoed":501},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundat